1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Declaring a struct creates a type, not an object. The type fixes one offset for every member.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,000
struct Ournode { char x, y, z; } holds three bytes at offsets 0, 1 and 2.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,000
sizeof is 3, and member order is byte order. GATE CSE 2018 Q2 reads those bytes through a char pointer.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Replace one char with an int and the offsets stop being 0, 1, 2. Something else is deciding them.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Alignment is a rule about addresses: an object whose size is 4 must begin at a multiple of 4.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
On x86-64, char is 1 byte, short 2, int and float 4, double, long and pointers 8. Alignment equals size.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,000
An int at address 4 is aligned. At address 3 it would straddle a four-byte boundary, so it is never placed there.

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Padding is how the compiler obeys the rule: it skips the bytes it cannot use.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Rule 1: round the running offset up to the member's alignment. Rule 2: place the member. Rule 3: round the total up.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
char c sits at 0, so the offset becomes 1. An int must start at 4, so three bytes are skipped.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:24,000
After the int the offset is 8, and the second char sits at 8 with no padding before it.

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The members end at 9. Rounding up to a multiple of 4 gives 12, so 6 of the 12 bytes are padding.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
C fixes the order of members, never their offsets. The compiler may pad between them as it likes.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:46,000
char, int, char is 12. char, char, int is 8. int, char, char is 8.

15
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Every one of the six permutations was laid out by the same function and measured, not assumed.

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Putting the widest member first usually shrinks the struct, because the small ones then pack together.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
A struct member is laid out like any other object. Its alignment is the largest alignment among its own members.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:16,000
struct Inner { char a; int b; } is 8 bytes: a at 0, three pad, b at 4. Its alignment is 4.

19
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:24,000
struct Outer { char c; struct Inner in; } puts c at 0 and must reach offset 4 before placing in.

20
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The outer block is 12 bytes: 1 + 3 pad + 8, and 12 is already a multiple of 4.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
In an array of structs the stride is sizeof, so element k starts at base + k times sizeof.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
struct P { char c; int i; } is 8 bytes. Ten of them occupy 80 bytes, not 50.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Padding is per element. It cannot be shared, because every element must satisfy the rule on its own.

24
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
If the first element is aligned, every later element is too, because the stride is a multiple of the alignment.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
A union is the opposite arrangement: every member starts at offset 0 and they overlap completely.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Its size is the largest member, rounded up to the largest alignment. union { char s[5]; int i; } is 8.

27
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:24,000
GATE CSE 2000 Q1.17: short s[5] is 10 bytes, the union of float and long is 8, so ignoring alignment the answer is 18.

28
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
A struct stacks its members; a union overlaps them. Same keyword shape, opposite arithmetic.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Because members overlap, writing one and reading another reinterprets the same bytes.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:46,000
0x12345678 read as four chars gives 78 56 34 12 on a little-endian machine.

31
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:54,000
The same write on a big-endian machine gives 12 34 56 78. Identical C, different output.

32
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Reading through a char pointer is always allowed. Reading a different non-character member reinterprets an object representation.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
An enumeration names integer constants. It stores no strings and adds no check on assignment.

34
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
enum colour { RED, GREEN, BLUE } gives 0, 1 and 2, because an unwritten first value is 0 and each next is one more.

35
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:24,000
The underlying type is implementation-defined but must hold every value. For a small enum on x86-64 it is int, so sizeof is 4.

36
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Two enumerators may share a value, and an enum variable may hold a value no enumerator names.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
A struct may contain a pointer to its own type, because a pointer has a fixed size known in advance.

38
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:46,000
struct node { int elt; struct node *next; } is 16 bytes: 4 for the int, 4 of padding, 8 for the pointer.

39
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:53,000
It may not contain itself by value: that would need its own size before it had one.

40
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
struct node *s[10] is an array of ten pointers, 80 bytes, and not ten nodes. GATE CSE 2000 Q1.31 asks exactly this.

